        "title": "View and Text",
        "theory": "## The Building Blocks\nIf you come from web development, you are used to using HTML tags like `<div>`, `<span>`, and `<h1>`.\n\nIn React Native, there is no HTML or DOM. You cannot use a `<div>`. Instead, React Native provides primitive mobile components:\n- `<View>` is the fundamental container (the equivalent of a `div`). It supports layout using Flexbox.\n- `<Text>` is the only component that can contain text (like a `p` or `span`). If you try to put raw text inside a `<View>`, the app will crash.\n- `<Image>` displays graphics.\n\n```jsx\n<View style={{ flex: 1, justifyContent: 'center' }}>\n  <Text style={{ fontSize: 24 }}>Hello Mobile World!</Text>\n</View>\n```\nAll styling is done using JavaScript objects that look almost identical to CSS, making the transition for web developers extremely fast.",
