        "title": "Routing and Navigation",
        "theory": "## The Navigator 2.0\nRouting in Flutter is managed by the `Navigator` widget, which manages a stack of Route objects.\n\nFor simple apps, you can use **Imperative Routing**:\n```dart\n// Push a new screen on top of the stack\nNavigator.push(context, MaterialPageRoute(builder: (context) => DetailsScreen()));\n\n// Pop the current screen off the stack (go back)\nNavigator.pop(context);\n```\nHowever, for complex apps (especially Flutter Web apps where you need the browser back button to work perfectly), you use the more complex **Navigator 2.0 (Declarative Routing)** via packages like `go_router`. This defines routes as a strict tree of URLs, making deep-linking (opening the app directly to a specific product page from an email link) incredibly robust.",
        "instructions": "## Task: Push a Route\nWrite the command used to push a new screen onto the navigation stack.",
