        "title": "User Input",
        "theory": "## Getting Input from Users\nThe `input()` function pauses the program and waits for the user to type something.\n\n```python\nname = input('What is your name? ')\nprint(f'Hello, {name}!')\n\n# input() always returns a string!\nage = int(input('How old are you? '))\nprint(f'Next year you will be {age + 1}')\n```\n\n**Important:** `input()` always returns a string. Convert it with `int()` or `float()` if you need a number.\n\n**What this code does:**\n- `input(...)`: Prints the prompt to the screen and waits for the user to type something and press Enter.\n- `name = ...`: Stores whatever the user typed into the `name` variable.\n- `f'Hello, {name}!'`: An f-string (formatted string) that injects the variable's value directly into the text.\n- `int(...)`: Takes the string returned by `input()` and converts it into a mathematical integer.",
